WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.010 --> 00:00:04.740
In this talk.

00:00:04.740 --> 00:00:06.180
I want to give you two things.

00:00:06.180 --> 00:00:08.720
One, a systematic way to think
about building uncertainty

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:09.780
visualizations.

00:00:09.780 --> 00:00:11.490
And, two, a taste
for how to do it.

00:00:11.490 --> 00:00:14.420
Using ggdist. ggdist is
an R package that

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:18.450
extends ggplot2 to provide
three additional geometries.

00:00:18.450 --> 00:00:20.030
Each of these is a
composite geometry

00:00:20.030 --> 00:00:22.378
that uses the same
underlying framework

00:00:22.378 --> 00:00:23.670
to understand how to use these.

00:00:23.670 --> 00:00:25.130
It's really helpful
to step back and think

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:26.600
about how to
construct uncertainty

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:28.260
visualization systematically.

00:00:28.260 --> 00:00:31.340
The idea behind ggdist is that first
you would derive an uncertainty

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.537
distribution, and
I don't really care

00:00:33.537 --> 00:00:35.120
if you're a Bayesian
or a frequentist,

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:36.180
it doesn't really matter.

00:00:36.180 --> 00:00:38.210
The point is you derive an
uncertainty distribution

00:00:38.210 --> 00:00:40.793
that you can represent in some
way that ggdist understands,

00:00:40.793 --> 00:00:42.230
which might be a
bunch of samples,

00:00:42.230 --> 00:00:44.642
it might be a
distributional vector,

00:00:44.642 --> 00:00:46.350
it could be a number
of different things.

00:00:46.350 --> 00:00:48.120
I'm not going to get
into the details of that.

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.370
But if you've derived the
uncertainty of a distribution,

00:00:50.370 --> 00:00:53.000
what you can then do is map
distributional properties

00:00:53.000 --> 00:00:55.790
onto visual channels, which
is what ggplot kind of calls

00:00:55.790 --> 00:00:56.427
aesthetics.

00:00:56.427 --> 00:00:58.010
This density plot
you have on the left

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.570
might be taking the
density function

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.550
and mapping it onto the
thickness of this slab.

00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:04.140
And you combine it
with an interval,

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:06.140
which might be, say,
taking two quintiles

00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:10.340
and mapping them on to the x-min
and x-max of the interval sub

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:11.130
geometry.

00:01:11.130 --> 00:01:14.630
And this is exactly how ggdist 
thinks about uncertainty

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:15.300
visualization.

00:01:15.300 --> 00:01:16.883
You have stats lab
interval and you're

00:01:16.883 --> 00:01:18.690
saying I want to map the PDF.

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.898
So the density function onto
the thickness aesthetic,

00:01:20.898 --> 00:01:23.023
you can then play around
with different properties,

00:01:23.023 --> 00:01:24.800
like maybe the
orientation of this slab

00:01:24.800 --> 00:01:26.810
relative to the
interval, you might

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.330
decide that you want the
CDF instead of the PDF,

00:01:29.330 --> 00:01:31.358
you might decide that
you want a gradient plot.

00:01:31.358 --> 00:01:32.900
So you map the
density onto the alpha

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:35.070
instead of onto the
thickness of the slab.

00:01:35.070 --> 00:01:39.777
In fact, as of R 4.1 ggdist
supports the true gradients

00:01:39.777 --> 00:01:41.610
that have been added
to the graphics engine.

00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:45.233
So if you output this
to say an SVG or a PDF,

00:01:45.233 --> 00:01:46.400
you can zoom all the way in.

00:01:46.400 --> 00:01:48.170
You'll get high
quality gradients

00:01:48.170 --> 00:01:49.800
no matter how far you Zoom in.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.520
We can create a whole variety
of different geometries

00:01:52.520 --> 00:01:55.230
just from this same slab
interval family.

00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:57.500
ggdist provides a bunch
of shortcut geometries,

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:00.407
but they're all really derived
from the same core geometry.

00:02:00.407 --> 00:02:02.240
Of course, the dots
interval and line ribbon

00:02:02.240 --> 00:02:03.470
are similarly flexible.

00:02:03.470 --> 00:02:05.720
You can do a bunch of cool
different things with them,

00:02:05.720 --> 00:02:06.860
but I don't really
have time to get

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:08.539
into the details
of how you would do

00:02:08.539 --> 00:02:10.020
all of those different things.

00:02:10.020 --> 00:02:11.990
On the other hand, I
did promise Twitter

00:02:11.990 --> 00:02:14.450
that this talk would be
a Twitter thread directly

00:02:14.450 --> 00:02:15.235
into your skull.

00:02:15.235 --> 00:02:16.610
So for the rest
of this talk, I'm

00:02:16.610 --> 00:02:18.440
just going to jump through a
whole bunch of different fun

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:19.140
examples.

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:21.320
I'm not going to have time to
tell you how to do any of them,

00:02:21.320 --> 00:02:22.987
but hopefully it will
wet your appetite

00:02:22.987 --> 00:02:24.600
and you might go and try it out.

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:25.440
So let's do this.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:25.940
All right.

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:28.910
So here's an example of
visualizing a Poisson

00:02:28.910 --> 00:02:30.860
distribution using a
distribution vector

00:02:30.860 --> 00:02:32.278
from the distributional package.

00:02:32.278 --> 00:02:34.070
It knows that it's a
discrete distribution,

00:02:34.070 --> 00:02:36.270
so it gives you a histogram
instead of a density plot.

00:02:36.270 --> 00:02:37.895
And it also allows
you to, for example,

00:02:37.895 --> 00:02:40.310
map the probability
onto the color

00:02:40.310 --> 00:02:42.453
so you can get this nice
little gradient effect

00:02:42.453 --> 00:02:44.370
speaking of gradients,
here's a gradient plot.

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.430
This also shows the use
of the dodge position

00:02:46.430 --> 00:02:48.600
from base ggplot with ggdist.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.343
Maybe you decide that you
want to map the CDF onto it.

00:02:51.343 --> 00:02:53.010
Actually, this is the
complimentary CDF.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:55.070
You can think about this
as an infinite number

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:57.800
of bar charts with
infinitesimal opacity

00:02:57.800 --> 00:02:59.280
all stacked on
top of each other.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.730
Other things that you
can do with mapping

00:03:01.730 --> 00:03:04.590
aesthetics at the
sub-slab level.

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:06.680
So here what we're
doing is we're saying,

00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:08.700
oh, there's a particular
region of interest.

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:12.390
Maybe we're interested
in 0 plus or minus 1.5.

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:15.470
So we just map that region
onto a different color,

00:03:15.470 --> 00:03:17.750
ggdist subdivides the
geometry and then allows

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:21.530
you to actually map colors
onto subparts of the geometry.

00:03:21.530 --> 00:03:23.450
Speaking of subdividing
the slab here,

00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:25.250
we're actually using
the CDF function,

00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:28.130
essentially bending the CDF
in order to show 50 to 80

00:03:28.130 --> 00:03:31.130
and confidence intervals
on the color of the slab.

00:03:31.130 --> 00:03:35.180
So this is a nice example of
using PDF and the CDF functions

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:37.250
together in order to
construct a geometry that

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.770
would be difficult to do
if you weren't able to use

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:41.600
both of them simultaneously.

00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.720
Another kind of complex
example of multiple functions

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:47.670
being mapped onto the
slab simultaneously.

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:50.952
The fill color is
actually varying by group.

00:03:50.952 --> 00:03:52.910
Then we're using the fill
ramp aesthetic, which

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:56.810
is an aesthetic within ggdist
that allows you to independently

00:03:56.810 --> 00:04:00.050
ramp the color to white
as the color gets closer

00:04:00.050 --> 00:04:02.870
to 0. Speaking of doing
crazy things with densities,

00:04:02.870 --> 00:04:04.670
any probable list
will tell you if you

00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:07.130
start taking density functions
and transforminging them

00:04:07.130 --> 00:04:09.080
and you have to correct
for the derivative

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.333
of the transformation.

00:04:10.333 --> 00:04:12.000
Here we have a log,
normal distribution.

00:04:12.000 --> 00:04:13.530
We look at it on the raw scale.

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.640
It looks like normal.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:16.760
If you plot this
on a log scale, it

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:18.420
should look like a
normal distribution.

00:04:18.420 --> 00:04:20.300
And in fact, it does look
like a normal distribution

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:22.130
because ggdist just
figures out the derivative

00:04:22.130 --> 00:04:24.510
and it applies the appropriate
correction to the density.

00:04:24.510 --> 00:04:26.385
Everyone loves rain
cloud plots these days.

00:04:26.385 --> 00:04:28.260
It just makes them
incredibly easy to create.

00:04:28.260 --> 00:04:30.380
So we're just combining
a slab geometry

00:04:30.380 --> 00:04:32.100
with a dots interval geometry.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:35.600
This works because the dots
interval geometry in ggdist

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:38.390
automatically figures
out how big the dots need

00:04:38.390 --> 00:04:39.450
to be in order to fit.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.200
This makes it really easy
to combine the dots geometry

00:04:42.200 --> 00:04:44.220
with other, more
complex plot layouts.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:46.220
So here, for example,
this is what some people

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:47.430
call a logic dot plot.

00:04:47.430 --> 00:04:48.710
Anyway, I hope that
would convince you

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:50.910
that there's a lot of cool
stuff you can do with ggdist.

00:04:50.910 --> 00:04:52.952
I haven't even really
scratched the surface here,

00:04:52.952 --> 00:04:54.780
So please give ggdist a try.

00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:56.840
And for more of the
examples from this talk,

00:04:56.840 --> 00:04:59.590
check out the URL below.

